-- title:
--   Local factor of a weight-two newform at q ≠ p
-- statement:
--   Let $\mathcal{O}$ be a complete discrete valuation ring (a domain, complete for the adic topology of its maximal ideal) of characteristic zero with finite residue field, let $p$ be a prime whose image in $\mathcal{O}$ lies in the maximal ideal, and write $\overline{K}$ for the chosen algebraic closure of $\operatorname{Frac}\mathcal{O}$. Let $M_g$ be a nonzero natural number and $g$ a weight-two cusp form on $\Gamma_0(M_g)$ which is a newform, i.e. $g$ is a normalised eigenform ($a_1(g)=1$, $a_{mn}(g)=a_m(g)a_n(g)$ for coprime $m,n$, and the usual prime-power recursions $a_{\ell^{r+2}}=a_\ell a_{\ell^{r+1}}-\ell a_{\ell^{r}}$ for $\ell\nmid M_g$ and $a_{\ell^{r+2}}=a_\ell a_{\ell^{r+1}}$ for $\ell \mid M_g$, all coefficients being $q$-expansion coefficients), and no proper divisor $M \mid M_g$ carries a normalised eigenform of weight two on $\Gamma_0(M)$ agreeing with $g$ at all primes not dividing $M_g$. Let $\chi_g$ be a ring homomorphism to $\mathbb{C}$ from the Hecke algebra of level $M_g$ and weight $2$ with empty excluded set (the $\mathbb{Z}$-subalgebra of $\operatorname{End}_{\mathbb{C}}$ of the cusp form space generated by the operators $T_\ell$ for primes $\ell \nmid M_g$ and $U_\ell$ for primes $\ell \mid M_g$) such that $\chi_g(T_\ell) = a_\ell(g)$ for every prime $\ell \nmid M_g$, and let $\iota$ be a ring homomorphism from the range of $\chi_g$ into $\overline{K}$. Let $q$ be a prime with $q \neq p$. Then three assertions hold: if $q^2 \mid M_g$ then $a_q(g) = 0$; if $q \mid M_g$ but $q^2 \nmid M_g$ then there is an integer $a$ equal to $1$ or $-1$ with $a_q(g) = a$; and if $q \nmid M_g$ then, writing $A = \iota(\chi_g(T_q))$, no root $x$ in $\overline{K}$ of $X^2 - A X + q$ admits a monic $R \in \mathcal{O}[X]$ with $R(x) = 0$ whose reduction modulo the maximal ideal is $X^{\deg R}$ (i.e. no root is residually zero).
--
--   These are the standard facts about the local factor at $q$ of a weight-two newform on $\Gamma_0(M_g)$: vanishing of $a_q$ when $q^2$ divides the level (Atkin–Lehner, Li), the Atkin–Lehner sign $a_q = \pm 1$ when $q$ exactly divides the level, and, for $q$ prime to the level, integrality of the two Frobenius roots together with $\alpha\beta = q \in \mathcal{O}^\times$, which prevents either from reducing to $0$. The statement feeds the comparison of local Euler factors at primes $q \neq p$ used in the level-lowering part of the argument, and is cited by [`CuspForm.IsNewform.sum_rootMultiplicity_residual_zero_eq_one_of_sq_dvd_of_ne`](thm.html#CuspForm.IsNewform.sum_rootMultiplicity_residual_zero_eq_one_of_sq_dvd_of_ne).
